Step 1: Check Overall CPU Usage

Command:

top

Example Output:

top - 10:30:15

Tasks: 250 total
%Cpu(s): 98.5 us, 1.0 sy, 0.5 id

PID   USER   %CPU   COMMAND
2548  mysql   95.2  mysqld
1234  root     2.1  sshd
Explanation
%Cpu(s): 98.5 us → CPU is almost fully utilized.
mysqld is using 95.2% CPU.
This indicates the MySQL process is causing the high CPU usage.

Step 2: Find the Top CPU-Consuming Processes

Command:

ps -eo pid,user,%cpu,%mem,comm --sort=-%cpu | head

Example Output:

PID   USER   %CPU  %MEM COMMAND
2548  mysql  95.2   25   mysqld
3210  root    5.0    2   java
1100  root    1.2    1   sshd
Explanation
PID → Process ID
USER → Owner of the process
%CPU → CPU usage
COMMAND → Process name

Now you know exactly which process is consuming CPU.

step 3
*********
to find how much core cpu is using

lscpu (Most Common)

Command:

lscpu

Example Output:

Architecture:        x86_64
CPU(s):              8
Core(s) per socket:  4
Socket(s):           2
Thread(s) per core:  1
Explanation
CPU(s): 8 → Total logical CPUs available to Linux.
Core(s) per socket): 4 → Each physical CPU has 4 cores.

That means only 4 processes can run simultaneously.
